I'm joined today by Deborah SATs who's  professor of philosophy at Stanford  University she's the author of numerous  articles and several books including why  some things should not be for sale the  moral limits of markets this is a super  interesting talk topic to me Deborah and  we've talked for example about how even  a believer in so-called capitalism  generally should be able to recognize  that there are some areas of life that  are not best left to the free market for  example I think health care is under  that umbrella health care in an economic  sense does not have elastic demand  there's effectively zero demand for most  health services by most people until  they all of a sudden have a condition or  disease that requires those services and  then all of a sudden they really need  access to those services so I've made  that case economically you deal more  with the sort of morality and philosophy  of it so talk to us about how we can  sort of think about the moral limits of  markets generally so so first of all I  think some of the economic arguments  those selves have moral dimension so you  know in thinking about inefficiency  efficiency is partly a normative idea  you know why do we care about efficiency  why does it matter  but a lot of the reason markets are  inefficient is when they have third  party effects that's a case also of  health care  you are bad health can affect me like  you're not being vaccinated can affect  me but we need a moral theory to tell us  which third party effects matter or not  that said there are some markets which  even if they could be efficient in the  delivery of goods and services we still  think it's inappropriate to market those  kinds of goods the most obvious one is  slavery nobody i think should object to  slavery because it's an inefficient  system with the idea that if it was  efficient it would be acceptable right  we think that it's simply  you know these are the human beings are  not the kinds of things that should be  bought and sold and there are other  items human hearts might be another one  we don't think people should be able to  sell their hearts even if it's the case  that two people would want to do to make  an exchange  same thing with slavery even if people  wanted to sell themselves into slavery  we don't think that the state ought to  enforce those kinds of contracts and I  think that's another important point  about markets a lot of people forget  that markets are institutions that  depend on enforcement mechanisms and in  that way they're really the concern of  everybody they're not just bilateral  deals between two single individuals so  there's an analysis to be made which you  sort of started to do with some of these  which relates to the the sort of way in  which the participants in the market  would be affected so slavery as an  example might be at the societal level  mathematically efficient in some way but  we would say that morally it would be  wrong to buy and sell people and us that  shouldn't be allowed there's also the  site of externalities right so when we  talk about the most efficient  organization of fossil fuel of  procurement and refinement and  distribution which might be the most  efficient way to get fuel from the  harvester through the refiner to the  end-user but there's all sorts of  negative externalities that we should  consider when we think about whether it  should be left to operate in the free  market can you talk a little bit about  negative externalities in this context  so economists of course think a lot  about negative externalities or positive  externalities so there are a lot of  market trades we're not just you know  people making the trade or affected but  third parties the obvious example would  be you and I make a arrangement but as  an effect of our trade pollution is  produced and people who were never  parties to that trade now bearing the  cost of pollution so that's a third part  effect in fact in life and in  interdependent societies third party  effects are everywhere you know if you  buy a shirt from the gap that I don't  like the color of that is a third party  effect on me so we need to pick out  which kinds of third party effects are  the ones we think are important enough  to regulate or in some cases even to  prevent a market from forming and that  again is where moral theory has to come  in because it has to tell us which are  the significant third party effects  something that might look like it's just  a two-party deal can have very  significant third party effects in my  book I give a case of if you think about  child labor as a as a market which it is  in many parts of the world  it obviously has direct effects on the  participants and we think there are lots  of reasons that children are not made  better off by being able to participate  in these markets but there's also what's  called a pecuniary externality there's  an effect on other prices if you have a  widespread child labor market you also  affect the prices of adult labor and by  making adult labor therefore cheaper  because it's now in competition you  increase the labor supply it means that  families they don't want to send their  children to work are going to be under  more compulsion to do so because the  adult wages aren't that high and that's  why Oh so just to get close this thought  in some cases closing off a market makes  other options available for people that  might not have been available had the  market existed so you can think about  wage minimum wage laws as something like  this they prevent some trades that would  be beneficial to the parties themselves  from forming there are probably some  people who  wanna work who will not be able to find  work once a minimum wage is in place on  the other hand there's a third party  effect of not having minimum wage laws  which is that the wages for everybody  are affected most of our audience is in  the United States and most of the  audience that is not in the United  States is in primarily what we would  call sort of Western democracies so I'd  be interested in hearing from you I  mentioned and on the program I've often  mentioned education as well as  healthcare as two areas of life that I  believe should be outside of the  so-called free market can you talk about  for the bulk of our audience what other  areas you believe should be outside of  that model as well so I'm gonna push  back a little bit the way you formulated  this because it's not a sharp inside or  outside  yeah and should be clear I mean I guess  I should also further clarify which is  that I don't even see it as free-market  or the opposite of free-market but  really I'm talking about regulation  right taking sort of real life into  consideration when we decide how free of  a market it should be right so yeah I  don't like the phrase free market in the  sense that I think all markets are  social inventions that are you know  depend on there being background  property rights enforcement mechanisms  social norms of trust and so all markets  are shot through with regulation it's  just a dream that there are these  completely unregulated markets now some  markets are less regulated than other  markets sure we can talk about degrees  of regulation it's a I think an  interesting question to ask what Goods  should never be in the market no matter  how much you could regulate them and you  know again the canonical example would  be slavery we think you know even a very  regulated slave market there's something  intrinsically objectionable about  treating human beings as if there  the kind of objects that should be for  sale some people think you know human  organs are another example unless I  would draw less a hard line on that but  I do think that there are lots of  reasons to not have if you allow some  kind of trading there's many many  reasons not to have a traditional market  with many buyers and many sellers for  different kinds of things so take an  organ mark you know organs if you were  ever going to try to incentivize people  to give up their organs the last thing  you would want would be a system which  distributed organs according to ability  to pay you know we think that human  kidneys ought to be distributed  according to need and so some one  society we only have one society in the  world where organ markets are legal at  the moment and that's around is a  monopsony where the government buys the  organs from people and distributes them  on the basis of need so that's a it's  not inside a market but it's not out  totally outside either there are other  forms of exchange my colleague at  Stanford al Roth has designed kind of  barter markets in organs where if I need  a kidney and the people who love me who  are willing to donate one are not  compatible and you need a kidney but you  have people who are not compatible we  can engage in a trade if people are  compatible with my people so that's not  a market exactly I mean there's no money  changing hands but it's using aspects of  a market right in order to more  efficiently distribute the kinds of  organs there are and some of these  matching you know barter markets with  organs now they've gone up to almost a  hundred people where they you know have  long chains of trade and that allows an  efficient dispersal of the kind of  organs they have so you know  I think the inside-outside I don't you  know or something is in a market or not  I think we just have to recognize that  you know mark not all markets are the  same and some kinds of markets raise  ethical problems that other markets  don't so organ markets raise lots of  ethical questions that an apple market  doesn't raise labor markets raise  problems that an apple market doesn't  raise you mentioned education and health  care  those raise ethical problems that other  markets don't raise for example in  education we're not just you know I'm  trying to trade goods we're trying to  produce citizens and so we have a lot to  to care about the effects of our you  know distributive arrangements on the  production of human citizens in an apple  market we're not trying to produce you  know citizen apples we're just trying to  get apples that are edible  so some markets we know tend to have  feedback mechanisms on the things that  are being exchanged and labor markets is  probably the best example of that you  know Adam Smith worried a lot about  workers who spend their whole lives as  appendages to machines not become being  good citizens right and so he argued  that we have to have state the state  playing an important role in education  we don't have to worry about Apple  markets producing you know you know  mindless sheep but we do have to worry  about what how what our education system  does or what our labor system does  because we're you know there's a  feedback loop between us in the market  Deborah the book is fascinating it's  called why some things should not be for  sale the moral limits of markets we are  recommending in and linking to it for  our audience we've been speaking with  the book's author Deborah sat's who  teaches philosophy at Stanford  University thank you so much for talking  to me about this and I'd love to have  you back and go go deeper in some of  these specific areas  well thank you it's great to talk to you

